The most direct way to fully understand Promise concurrency and chaining is to hand-write a `Promise.all` implementation yourself:

```javascript
// A minimal hand-written Promise.all implementation
function promiseAll(promises) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    let results = [];
    let completedCount = 0;

    promises.forEach((p, index) => {
      // Make sure every item passed in is wrapped as a Promise
      Promise.resolve(p).then(res => {
        results[index] = res; // keep the original array order
        completedCount++;
        if (completedCount === promises.length) {
          resolve(results);   // only resolve once everything has succeeded
        }
      }).catch(reject);       // reject immediately if even one fails
    });
  });
}
```

> 💡 **Key thing to notice**: `Promise.all` is extremely efficient for firing off parallel network requests, but be aware of its "short-circuit" behavior — if any single Promise throws, the whole thing immediately rejects. Understanding its internal counter and result-index binding is a fundamental skill for frontend interviews and async performance tuning.

